re-extraction-for-style-transferring">Feature Extraction</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch-gram-matrix-for-style-transferring">Gram Matrix</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch-optimization-process-for-style-transferring">Optimization Process</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading9"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse9" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse9" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Interview Questions</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading9" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse9"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch-interview-questions">Pytorch Interview</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading10"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse10" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse10" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Misc</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading10" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse10"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/gpu-acceleration-in-pytorch">GPU Acceleration in PyTorch</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch-profiler">PyTorch Profiler</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-save-a-pytorch-model">How to Save a PyTorch Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/torch-cuda-in-pytorch">torch.Cuda in PyTorch</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/torch-random-in-pytorch">torch.random in PyTorch</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/torch-random-tensor">Torch Random Tensor</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="how-to-save-a-pytorch-model">next →</a> <a class="next" href="gpu-acceleration-in-pytorch">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">PyTorch Profiler</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>28 Mar 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 9 min read</span></div><img alt="PyTorch Profiler" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/pytorch/images/pytorch-profiler.png"/><h2 class="h2">Introduction</h2><p>PyTorch, an open-source device gaining knowledge of libraries developed by using Facebook's AI Research lab, has turned out to be a popular preference for both instructional studies and industry applications. Its flexibility and dynamic computation graph approach have made it a favorite amongst <a href="deep-learning">deep learning</a> practitioners. One of the important aspects of growing efficient device studying fashions is the potential to profile and optimize them. <a href="pytorch">PyTorch</a> presents a powerful device for this cause: `torch. Profiler`.</p><h2 class="h2">1. Introduction to Profiling</h2><p>Profiling is the procedure of measuring the performance of a program, identifying the parts of the code that devour the most sources, and understanding wherein optimizations may be applied. In the context of deep mastering, profiling enables the identification of bottlenecks within the model education and inference tactics. These bottlenecks can stem from numerous resources, which include inefficient code, suboptimal use of hardware resources, or reminiscence issues.</p><p><strong>Profiling is critical because it permits builders to:</strong></p><ul class="points"><li>Improve the performance and performance of their fashions.</li><li>Reduce training and inference time.</li><li>Optimize the usage of computational resources.</li><li>Gain insights into the conduct in their fashions.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">2. Overview of `torch.Profiler`</h2><p>`torch.Profiler` is a powerful and bendy tool supplied with the aid of PyTorch to facilitate the profiling of deep learning fashions. It gives a wide variety of functions to help developers apprehend the overall performance characteristics in their models and optimize them for that reason. Some of the important thing functions of `torch.Profiler` encompass:</p><ul class="points"><li>Detailed overall performance metrics: `torch.Profiler` offers exact data about the time spent in special parts of the version, which includes individual operations, layers, and custom activities. Support for GPU profiling: It supports profiling on both CPUs and GPUs, making it suitable for a wide range of hardware configurations.</li><li>Integration with TensorBoard: `torch.Profiler` can export profiling records to TensorBoard, taking into account visual evaluation and higher insights. Memory profiling: It includes functions for profiling memory utilization, helping builders understand and optimize their models' memory footprints.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">3. Installation and Setup</h2><p>To use `torch.profiler`, you need to have PyTorch installed. If you haven't installed PyTorch yet, you can do so using the following command:</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> 
pip install torch   
</textarea></div><p>Once PyTorch is hooked up, you can start using `torch.Profiler`. There are no additional applications required for basic profiling; however, for superior capabilities like TensorBoard integration, you need to install TensorBoard as well:</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> 
pip install tensorboard   
</textarea></div><h2 class="h2">4. Basic Usage</h2><h3 class="h3">Profiling a Simple Model</h3><p>To reveal the primary utilization of `torch.Profiler, ' we can start by profiling an easy neural network version. We'll use a simple feedforward neural community for this reason.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> 
import torch
import torch.nn as nn
import torch.optim as optim
from torch.profiler import profile, record_function, ProfilerActivity
class SimpleNN(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super(SimpleNN, self).__init__()
        self.fc1 = nn.Linear(784, 256)
        self.fc2 = nn.Linear(256, 128)
        self.fc3 = nn.Linear(128, 10)

    def forward(self, x):
        x = torch.relu(self.fc1(x))
        x = torch.relu(self.fc2(x))
        x = self.fc3(x)
        return x
model = SimpleNN()
criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()
optimizer = optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.01)
inputs = torch.randn(64, 784)
labels = torch.randint(0, 10, (64,))
def train_step(inputs, labels):
    optimizer.zero_grad()
    outputs = model(inputs)
    loss = criterion(outputs, labels)
    loss.backward()
    optimizer.step()
with profile(activities=[ProfilerActivity.CPU, ProfilerActivity.CUDA], record_shapes=True) as prof:
    with record_function("model_training"):
        train_step(inputs, labels)
print(prof.key_averages().table(sort_by="cpu_time_total", row_limit=10))   
</textarea></div><p>In this case, we define an easy feedforward neural network with three absolutely connected layers. We then create dummy input facts and outline a training step function. Using `torch.Profiler`, we profile the training step and print the profiling effects.</p><h3 class="h3">Analyzing the Profiler Output</h3><p>The output of the profiler gives exact data on approximately the time spent in distinctive parts of the code. The `prof.Key_averages().Desk()` approach generates a summary table that shows the average time spent on each operation, taken care of by way of the entire CPU time.</p><p>The desk includes the following columns:</p><ul class="points"><li>Name: The call of the operation.</li><li>Self CPU total: The general time spent on the CPU for the operation itself.</li><li>CPU overall: The total time spent at the CPU for the operation and its baby operations.</li><li>CPU time avg: The common time spent on the CPU per operation.</li><li>CUDA Overall: The overall time spent at the GPU for the operation and its child operations (if applicable).</li><li>Number of Calls: There was a variety of instances when the operation was called.</li></ul><p>By analyzing this output, you could discover the operations that consume the most time and can require optimization.</p><h2 class="h2">5. Advanced Features</h2><h3 class="h3">Custom Events</h3><p>`torch.profiler` allows you to add custom events to the profiling output using the `record_function` context manager. This is useful for profiling specific parts of your code that are not captured by default.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> 
with profile(activities=[ProfilerActivity.CPU, ProfilerActivity.CUDA], record_shapes=True) as prof:
    with record_function("custom_event"):
        # Your code here
        train_step(inputs, labels)   
</textarea></div><p>By adding custom events, you can get more granular insights into the performance of your code.</p><h3 class="h3">Using TensorBoard with `torch.profiler`</h3><p><a href="tensorflow-tensorboard">TensorBoard</a> is a powerful tool for visualizing and analyzing profiling data. `torch.profiler` can export profiling data to TensorBoard, allowing you to leverage its visualization capabilities.</p><p>First, you need to set up TensorBoard in your project:</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> 
from torch.profiler import profile, record_function, ProfilerActivity, tensorboard_trace_handler

# Use tensorboard_trace_handler to save profiling data
with profile(activities=[ProfilerActivity.CPU, ProfilerActivity.CUDA],
             schedule=torch.profiler.schedule(wait=1, warmup=1, active=3, repeat=2),
             on_trace_ready=tensorboard_trace_handler("./logdir")) as prof:
    for _ in range(8):
        with record_function("model_training"):
            train_step(inputs, labels)
        prof.step()   
</textarea></div><p>In this example, we use the `tensorboard_trace_handler` to save the profiling data to the `./logdir` directory. You can then launch TensorBoard to visualize the profiling data:</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> 
tensorboard --logdir=./logdir   
</textarea></div><p>Open the provided <a href="url">URL</a> in your <a href="browsers">browser</a> to explore the profiling data.</p><h3 class="h3">Memory Profiling</h3><p>Memory profiling is essential for the know-how of the reminiscence utilization of your fashions and figuring out capability memory bottlenecks. `torch.Profiler` consists of capabilities for profiling memory usage, which can be enabled using the `profile_memory=True` alternative.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> 
with profile(activities=[ProfilerActivity.CPU, ProfilerActivity.CUDA], profile_memory=True, record_shapes=True) as prof:
    with record_function("model_training"):
        train_step(inputs, labels)

# Print the memory profiling results
print(prof.key_averages().table(sort_by="self_cuda_memory_usage", row_limit=10))   
</textarea></div><p>The memory profiling output includes additional columns such as:</p><ul class="points"><li>Self CUDA memory total: The total memory usage on the GPU for the operation itself.</li><li>CUDA memory total: The total memory usage on the GPU for the operation and its child operations.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">6. Profiling Best Practices</h2><h3 class="h3">Reducing Overhead</h3><p>Profiling introduces a few overheads, which could affect the performance of your code. To minimize this overhead, don't forget the subsequent satisfactory practices:</p><p>Profile the essential elements of your code best: Use the `record_function` context supervisor to profile particular components of your code rather than the complete program.</p><ul class="points"><li>Use suitable profiling schedules: Use the `torch.Profiler.Time table` to control when profiling begins and stops, decreasing the impact on your standard performance.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Efficient Use of Resources</h3><p>To efficaciously use computational resources at the same time as profiling, follow the pointers:</p><p>Profile at the goal hardware: Perform profiling at the identical hardware on which you plan to install your model directly to get accurate overall performance insights.</p><ul class="points"><li>Analyze both CPU and GPU overall performance: Ensure that you profile each CPU and GPU sports to get an entire photograph of your model's overall performance.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">7. Practical Examples</h2><h3 class="h3">Profiling a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)</h3><p>Let's profile an easy CNN model to demonstrate how `torch.Profiler` can be used for extra-complex architectures.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> 
import torch.nn as nn

# Define a simple CNN model
class SimpleCNN(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super(SimpleCNN, self).__init__()
        self.conv1 = nn.Conv2d(1, 32, kernel_size=3, stride=1, padding=1)
        self.conv2 = nn.Conv2d(32, 64, kernel_size=3, stride=1, padding=1)
        self.fc1 = nn.Linear(64*28*28, 128)
        self.fc2 = nn.Linear(128, 10)

    def forward(self, x):
        x = torch.relu(self.conv1(x))
        x = torch.max_pool2d(x, 2)
        x = torch.relu(self.conv2(x))
        x = torch.max_pool2d(x, 2)
        x = x.view(-1, 64*28*28)
        x = torch.relu(self.fc1(x))
        x = self.fc2(x)
        return x

# Create a model instance and define loss function and optimizer
model = SimpleCNN()
criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()
optimizer = optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.01)

# Generate dummy input data
inputs = torch.randn(64, 1, 28, 28)
labels = torch.randint(0, 10, (64,))

# Define a training step function
def train_step(inputs, labels):
    optimizer.zero_grad()
    outputs = model(inputs)
    loss = criterion(outputs, labels)
    loss.backward()
    optimizer.step()

# Use torch.profiler to profile the training step
with profile(activities=[ProfilerActivity.CPU, ProfilerActivity.CUDA], record_shapes=True) as prof:
    with record_function("model_training"):
        train_step(inputs, labels)

# Print the profiling results
print(prof.key_averages().table(sort_by="cpu_time_total", row_limit=10))   
</textarea></div><p>In this example, we define a simple <a href="pytorch-convolutional-neural-network">CNN</a> model with two convolutional layers and two fully connected layers. We then profile the training step using `torch.profiler` and print the profiling results.</p><h3 class="h3">Profiling a Recurrent Neural Network (RNN)</h3><p>Next, let's profile a simple <a href="recurrent-neural-network-in-tensorflow">RNN</a> model to demonstrate profiling for sequence-based architectures.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"> 
import torch.nn as nn

# Define a simple RNN model
class SimpleRNN(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super(SimpleRNN, self).__init__()
        self.rnn = nn.RNN(input_size=10, hidden_size=20, num_layers=2, batch_first=True)
        self.fc = nn.Linear(20, 10)

    def forward(self, x):
        h0 = torch.zeros(2, x.size(0), 20)
        out, _ = self.rnn(x, h0)
        out = self.fc(out[:, -1, :])
        return out

# Create a model instance and define loss function and optimizer
model = SimpleRNN()
criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()
optimizer = optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.01)

# Generate dummy input data
inputs = torch.randn(64, 5, 10)
labels = torch.randint(0, 10, (64,))

# Define a training step function
def train_step(inputs, labels):
    optimizer.zero_grad()
    outputs = model(inputs)
    loss = criterion(outputs, labels)
    loss.backward()
    optimizer.step()

# Use torch.profiler to profile the training step
with profile(activities=[ProfilerActivity.CPU, ProfilerActivity.CUDA], record_shapes=True) as prof:
    with record_function("model_training"):
        train_step(inputs, labels)

# Print the profiling results
print(prof.key_averages().table(sort_by="cpu_time_total", row_limit=10))   
</textarea></div><p>In this case, we define a easy RNN version with two recurrent layers and one fully connected layer. We then profile the education step using the `torch. Profiler` and print the profiling consequences.</p><h2 class="h2">8. Further Insights and Future Directions</h2><p>While `torch.Profiler` is an essential device for performance optimization, it is important to continuously stay up to date with the contemporary capabilities and upgrades furnished via the PyTorch development team. Profiling gear evolves swiftly to maintain pace with improvements in hardware and software program technology. Therefore, integrating ongoing getting-to-know and exploration into your workflow will ensure you leverage the full capability of `torch.Profiler`.</p><h3 class="h3">Profiling Distributed Training</h3><p>As models become more complex and long, allotted training becomes a need rather than a choice. Profiling allotted education setups can be more challenging because of the added layer of communication overhead between special nodes. `torch.Profiler` consists of support for profiling disbursed schooling situations, helping you comprehend not just the computational aspects but also the information transfer and synchronization fees.</p><p>To profile disbursed schooling, you can install `torch.Profiler` in a similar way to single-node training but ensure that you capture and analyze the communication events. This can highlight bottlenecks in network communication, imbalances in workload distribution, and inefficiencies in facts parallelism.</p><h3 class="h3">Integration with Other Profiling Tools</h3><p>While `torch.Profiler` presents a complete profiling solution, integrating it with different profiling and debugging tools can offer deeper insights. Tools such as NVIDIA's Nsight Systems, Intel's VTune Profiler, or Google Cloud's profiling gear can complement `torch.Profiler` by offering hardware-unique metrics and visualizations.</p><p>For instance, Nsight Systems can offer targeted GPU usage metrics and kernel-stage insights that may be critical for optimizing CUDA operations. Combining the high-level review from `torch.Profiler` with the low-level info from those specialized tools can create a holistic profiling strategy.</p><h3 class="h3">Automated Performance Tuning</h3><p>Looking forward, the combination of profiling gear with computerized overall performance tuning frameworks is a thrilling vicinity. Machine learning models can benefit from automatic hyperparameter tuning and structure seeking.</p><h3 class="h3">Profiling for Energy Efficiency</h3><p>As the environmental effect of large-scale AI fashions becomes a developing issue, profiling for strength performance is gaining significance. Understanding the energy consumption styles of your fashions can assist in optimizing not just for performance but also for energy utilization. While `torch.Profiler` focuses on time and reminiscence metrics, integrating it with power tracking equipment can offer insights into the electricity performance of different operations and fashions.</p><h3 class="h3">Continuous Profiling in Production</h3><p>Profiling has to not be constrained to the improvement phase. Continuous profiling in manufacturing environments can assist in figuring out and mitigating performance regressions, information real-international utilization styles, and ensuring that models perform optimally beneath varied conditions. Tools like PyTorch Profiler can be included in your production monitoring structures to offer ongoing insights.</p><h3 class="h3">Community and Collaboration</h3><p>The PyTorch network is a rich, useful resource for studying and collaboration. Engaging with the network through boards, <a href="github">GitHub</a> issues, and meetings can offer practical insights and guidelines that are not constantly blanketed in authentic documentation. Sharing your profiling experiences and optimization strategies can also contribute to the collective understanding, helping others inside the network.</p><h2 class="h2">8. Conclusion</h2><p>Profiling is a crucial factor in developing green and optimized deep knowledge of models. `torch.Profiler provides an effective and flexible toolset for profiling and analyzing the performance of PyTorch models. By leveraging its features, developers can gain valuable insights into the conduct of their fashions, identify overall performance bottlenecks, and observe optimizations to enhance standard efficiency.</p><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="how-to-save-a-pytorch-model">How-to-save-a-pytorch-model</a></span></div><br/> <div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="gpu-acceleration-in-pytorch" style="float:left">← prev</a> <a class="next" href="how-to-save-a-pytorch-model" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br/> </td></tr></tbody></table></div></body></html><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="torch-random-tensor"><h3 class="h3">Torch Random Tensor</h3><p class="related-post-content">Introduction to PyTorch and Tensors An open-source, highly configurable deep learning framework called PyTorch is used for activities including creating neural networks, training models, and carrying out machine learning operations. PyTorch, created by Facebook's AI Research lab, gives developers access to an interactive and dynamic interface...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 12 min read <span class="arrow-position"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1.3rem" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 512 512" width="1.3rem" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M295.6 163.7c-5.1 5-5.1 13.3-.1 18.4l60.8 60.9H124.9c-7.1 0-12.9 5.8-12.9 13s5.8 13 12.9 13h231.3l-60.8 60.9c-5 5.1-4.9 13.3.1 18.4 5.1 5 13.2 5 18.3-.1l82.4-83c1.1-1.2 2-2.5 2.7-4.1.7-1.6 1-3.3 1-5 0-